---
import PropertyPackPage from '@/components/property-pack/PropertyPackPage.astro';
import requiredProperties from '@/data/property-pack/required-properties.json';
import provenanceIndex from '../../../../source/03-standards/rml/provenance-index.json';
import {
  changedKindLexicalMatches,
  comparisonDimensions,
  continuityPoints,
  evidenceLinks,
  interpretationLimits,
  lexicalMatches,
  sameKindLexicalMatches,
  v1Counts,
  v2Counts,
} from '@/lib/model-comparison.mjs';

const kindLabel = (value) => value.replaceAll('-', ' ');
const provenancePaths = new Set(Object.values(provenanceIndex.leaves).map((entry) => entry.leaf_path));
const exactPathMatches = requiredProperties.filter((record) => provenancePaths.has(record.source.path)).length;
const readerText = (value) => String(value)
  .replaceAll('V1', 'PDTF 1.0')
  .replaceAll('current baseline', 'published implementation');
---
<PropertyPackPage
  title="PDTF 1.0 lineage"
  description="The proven and unresolved lineage from the PDTF 1.0 schema and extracted ontology to the SPDTF 2.0 Property Pack ontology candidate."
  category="Lineage and crosswalk"
  crumbs={[{ label: 'PDTF 1.0 lineage' }]}
>
  <h1>PDTF 1.0 lineage and semantic crosswalk</h1>

  <p class="lead v2-comparison-lead">
    PDTF 1.0 contains the Property Pack source definition in its JSON Schema and also
    has an ontology extracted from the schema, dictionary and glossary. The SPDTF 2.0
    Property Pack candidate reconsiders that same bounded profile by contextual meaning;
    it is not PDTF 1.0 with a revised list of terms.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--key" id="bottom-line">
    <p class="callout__label">Bottom line</p>
    <p>
      <strong>The main change is modelling authority and evidence discipline—not a move
      from JSON to RDF.</strong> PDTF 1.0 already provides a graph ontology. The Property Pack candidate asks domain-led
      questions afresh, keeps source facts separate from semantic proposals and assigns
      every candidate resource one explicit semantic home.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout callout--warn" id="crosswalk-status">
    <p class="callout__label">Crosswalk status</p>
    <p>
      <strong>All {requiredProperties.length} required Property Pack paths occur in the
      PDTF 1.0 schema.</strong> Only <strong>{exactPathMatches}</strong> have an exact
      full-path entry in the current ontology provenance index. Consolidation and
      one-to-many modelling mean this is not a semantic coverage percentage. The
      maintained 451-row PDTF 1.0 ontology crosswalk remains incomplete and must record
      the construct or gap and its retained, revised, consolidated, split or missing
      disposition.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p class="muted tiny">
    In this report, <strong>PDTF 1.0</strong> means the published schema implementation
    and its derived corpus, with each child artefact retaining its own status. The
    <strong>Property Pack ontology candidate</strong> is an accelerated SPDTF 2.0
    component—not an adoption, release or replacement claim.
  </p>


  <h2 id="scale">Quantitative snapshot</h2>
  <div class="callout callout--warn">
    <p class="callout__label">Not a like-for-like size comparison</p>
    <p>
      PDTF 1.0 covers the broader schema and overlays. The candidate covers only the required
      Property Pack ontology candidate and consolidates repeated source paths into semantic patterns.
      These figures describe each corpus; they do not rank quality or completeness.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="v2-snapshot-grid">
    <section class="v2-snapshot v2-snapshot--current">
      <header>
        <span class="pill pill--info">Published implementation</span>
        <h3>PDTF 1.0 · schema-derived ontology</h3>
        <p>One published PDTF namespace, organised through concern modules and industry views.</p>
      </header>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>{v1Counts.resources}</dt><dd>OWL resources</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{v1Counts.classes}</dt><dd>classes</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{v1Counts.objectProperties}</dt><dd>object properties</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{v1Counts.datatypeProperties}</dt><dd>datatype properties</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{v1Counts.schemes}</dt><dd>value schemes · {v1Counts.concepts} concepts</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{v1Counts.shapes}</dt><dd>SHACL shapes · {v1Counts.constraints} indexed constraints</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{v1Counts.overlayProfiles}</dt><dd>overlay profiles</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{v1Counts.exemplars}</dt><dd>diagnostic exemplars</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="v2-snapshot v2-snapshot--candidate">
      <header>
        <span class="pill pill--warn">Public review candidate</span>
        <h3>SPDTF 2.0 · Property Pack ontology candidate</h3>
        <p>A separate candidate namespace with explicit context ownership and source-item traceability.</p>
      </header>
      <dl>
        <div><dt>{v2Counts.resources}</dt><dd>OWL resources</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{v2Counts.classes}</dt><dd>classes</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{v2Counts.objectProperties}</dt><dd>object properties</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{v2Counts.datatypeProperties}</dt><dd>datatype properties</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{v2Counts.schemes}</dt><dd>value schemes · {v2Counts.concepts} concepts</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{v2Counts.shapes}</dt><dd>SHACL shapes · {v2Counts.constraints} property constraints</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{v2Counts.sourceItems}</dt><dd>required source data points</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{v2Counts.topicConcepts}</dt><dd>source-topic concepts</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2 id="comparison">Side-by-side comparison</h2>
  <div class="v2-compare" role="table" aria-label="PDTF 1.0 and Property Pack ontology candidate comparison">
    <div class="v2-compare__head" role="row">
      <span role="columnheader">Dimension</span>
      <span role="columnheader">PDTF 1.0 · published implementation</span>
      <span role="columnheader">Property Pack ontology · review candidate</span>
    </div>
    {comparisonDimensions.map((dimension) => (
      <section class="v2-compare__row" id={dimension.id} role="row">
        <h3 class="v2-compare__dimension" role="rowheader">{dimension.label}</h3>
        <div class="v2-compare__cell" role="cell">
          <span class="v2-compare__cell-label">PDTF 1.0 · published implementation</span>
          <p>{readerText(dimension.v1)}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="v2-compare__cell" role="cell">
          <span class="v2-compare__cell-label">Property Pack ontology · review candidate</span>
          <p>{readerText(dimension.v2)}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="v2-compare__implication" role="cell" aria-colspan="3">
          <strong>Why it matters</strong>
          <span>{readerText(dimension.implication)}</span>
        </div>
        <p class="v2-compare__evidence" role="cell" aria-colspan="3">
          Evidence:
          {dimension.evidence.map((item, index) => (
            <><a href={item.href}>{item.label}</a>{index < dimension.evidence.length - 1 ? ' · ' : ''}</>
          ))}
        </p>
      </section>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="semantic-deltas">Concrete semantic differences</h2>

  <div class="v2-note-grid">
    <section>
      <h3>Both models are graphs</h3>
      <p>
        PDTF 1.0 already moved beyond the JSON tree and made explicit identity, class and
        relationship decisions. The Property Pack candidate changes how those decisions are sourced and owned;
        it does not introduce graph modelling for the first time.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h3>The overlap is limited</h3>
      <p>
        Only <strong>{lexicalMatches.length}</strong> of {v2Counts.resources} candidate resources share
        an exact local name with a PDTF 1.0 resource. Only <strong>{sameKindLexicalMatches.length}</strong>
        also retain the same broad resource kind. Names alone do not establish equivalence.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="v2-name-comparison" aria-labelledby="same-kind-name-matches">
    <h3 id="same-kind-name-matches">Exact local-name matches with the same resource kind</h3>
    <p>
      This is a mechanical identifier check: each row uses the same local name in both
      models and is classified at the same broad level, such as a class or an object
      property. It identifies a useful starting point for crosswalk review; it does
      <strong>not</strong> establish that the definitions, identity rules, relationships or
      constraints mean the same thing.
    </p>
    <p class="v2-name-comparison__summary">
      <strong>{sameKindLexicalMatches.length} names to review.</strong> Compare the meaning and
      evidence behind each pair before treating it as continuity.
    </p>
    <div class="v2-name-matrix" role="table" aria-label="Exact local-name matches with the same resource kind">
      <div class="v2-name-matrix__head" role="row">
        <span role="columnheader">Local name</span>
        <span role="columnheader">PDTF 1.0</span>
        <span role="columnheader">Property Pack candidate</span>
      </div>
      {sameKindLexicalMatches.map((item) => (
        <div class="v2-name-matrix__row" role="row">
          <p class="v2-name-matrix__name" role="cell" data-label="Local name">
            <code>{item.localName}</code><span class="sr-only">{` ${kindLabel(item.v1Kind)}`}</span>
          </p>
          <span role="cell" data-label="PDTF 1.0">{kindLabel(item.v1Kind)}</span>
          <span role="cell" data-label="Property Pack candidate">{kindLabel(item.v2Kind)}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="v2-name-comparison" aria-labelledby="reclassified-name-matches">
    <h3 id="reclassified-name-matches">Exact local-name matches that were reclassified</h3>
    <p>
      These names also occur in both models, but their broad resource kind has changed.
      For example, a term may be a class in one model and a property in the other. This
      is a clear signal not to infer a mapping from spelling alone: the modelling role
      has already changed and needs an explicit decision.
    </p>
    <p class="v2-name-comparison__summary">
      <strong>{changedKindLexicalMatches.length} reclassification questions.</strong> Each row
      needs a decision on whether the two uses are related, distinct or simply coincidental.
    </p>
    <div class="v2-name-matrix" role="table" aria-label="Exact local-name matches with different resource kinds">
      <div class="v2-name-matrix__head" role="row">
        <span role="columnheader">Local name</span>
        <span role="columnheader">PDTF 1.0</span>
        <span role="columnheader">Property Pack candidate</span>
      </div>
      {changedKindLexicalMatches.map((item) => (
        <div class="v2-name-matrix__row" role="row">
          <p class="v2-name-matrix__name" role="cell" data-label="Local name">
            <code>{item.localName}</code><span class="sr-only">{` ${kindLabel(item.v1Kind)}`}</span>
          </p>
          <span role="cell" data-label="PDTF 1.0">{kindLabel(item.v1Kind)}</span>
          <span role="cell" data-label="Property Pack candidate">{kindLabel(item.v2Kind)}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="callout callout--warn">
    <p class="callout__label">A substantive identity question remains open</p>
    <p>
      PDTF 1.0 defines <a href="/pdtf/Property">Property</a> through spatial-material continuity
      and explicitly treats UPRN as a contingent identifier, not the identity criterion.
      The Property Pack ontology candidate <a href="/spdtf-2/property-pack/resources/common/Property">Property candidate</a> currently associates
      continuity with the same assigned UPRN. That is a semantic difference for working-group
      review—not a settled SPDTF 2.0 decision.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="v2-note-grid">
    <section>
      <h3>Present in PDTF 1.0, outside the initial Property Pack scope</h3>
      <p>
        Examples include Transaction, Person, Organisation, Buyer, Seller, Claim,
        Evidence and Trust Framework. Their absence from this candidate does not retire them; the
        451-item Property Pack ontology candidate simply does not yet justify every broader PDTF concept.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h3>Prominent Property Pack ontology candidate patterns</h3>
      <p>
        Boundary Statement, Disclosure Statement, Fixture Schedule, Register Extract,
        Search Finding, Utility Service Record and Data Sharing Authorisation make
        Property Pack meaning more explicit within proposed semantic homes.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2 id="continuity">What carries forward from PDTF 1.0</h2>
  <ul class="v2-check-list">
    {continuityPoints.map((item) => <li>{readerText(item)}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2 id="implications">What this means in practice</h2>
  <div class="v2-card-grid">
    <section class="v2-card">
      <span class="pill pill--info">Working groups</span>
      <h3>Review meaning, not ontology syntax</h3>
      <p>Test definitions, identity criteria, context ownership, relationships and constraints against real domain evidence.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="v2-card">
      <span class="pill pill--info">Current implementers</span>
      <h3>Keep using the current contracts</h3>
      <p>The Property Pack ontology candidate is not a replacement schema or migration target. Current integrations remain anchored to the published PDTF 1.0 implementation.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="v2-card">
      <span class="pill pill--info">Interoperability group</span>
      <h3>Challenge the Common boundary</h3>
      <p>Review genuinely shared meaning and propose mappings without overriding each bounded context's internal vocabulary.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="v2-card">
      <span class="pill pill--info">Model team</span>
      <h3>Make migration explicit</h3>
      <p>Record reviewed equivalence, transformation and projection decisions before generating replacement schemas or forms.</p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2 id="limits">What the comparison does not prove</h2>
  <ul class="v2-limit-list">
    {interpretationLimits.map((item) => <li>{readerText(item)}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2 id="evidence">Evidence and method</h2>
  <p>
    The figures are calculated at build time from the current generated ontology model,
    the committed PDTF 1.0 profile and exemplar directories, and the isolated Property Pack ontology candidate
    projections. The lexical comparison uses exact local names only and deliberately
    makes no equivalence claim. Narrative conclusions are constrained by the recorded
    scope and architecture decisions.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {evidenceLinks.map((item) => <li><a href={item.href}>{readerText(item.label)}</a></li>)}
  </ul>
</PropertyPackPage>
